---
title: Charred Salsa Verde
description: 'How would a chemical engineer make the world’s best Mexican-style salsa verde? The secret is to char your ingredients — this concentrates flavors and adds a smoky depth that most salsas don’t have. If you can’t find tomatillos, firm, not-quite-ripe red tomatoes work as a substitute.'
tags: Salsa, Mexican, Grilling, Condiment
---

Char the @tomatillos{480%g}(husks removed), @white onion{200%g}(quartered), @cilantro{20%g}, @jalapeños{1-3}(to taste) and @garlic{4-6%cloves}(peeled) on a #grill{} until well blackened in spots. This drives the Maillard reactions that give the salsa its smoky depth.

Let the charred ingredients cool slightly, then transfer to a #food processor{} and pulse until combined but still slightly chunky.

Season generously with @salt{½-1%tsp}. Store in a #container{} in the fridge. Serve with tortilla chips, or on quesadillas, tacos or nachos.
